00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:20.640
<v Norman Knudsen>If we don't get a late arrest. 1922 is back with you.
<v Norman Knudsen>如果我们没收到什么晚间逮捕呼叫。1922回到你们身边了。

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:22.370
<v Ira Glass>They head toward the door.
<v Ira Glass>他们走向门口。

00:51:22.370 --> 00:51:25.130
<v Norman Knudsen>Donna, Pete, we'll see you guys manana.
<v Norman Knudsen>唐娜，皮特，明天见，伙计们。

00:51:25.130 --> 00:51:33.140
<v Ira Glass>Hasta la bye-bye, says Donna, over by the counter.
<v Ira Glass>再见啦，唐娜在柜台边说。

00:51:33.140 --> 00:51:35.430
<v Norman Knudsen>All right, see you guys all later.
<v Norman Knudsen>好的，回头见，伙计们。

00:51:35.430 --> 00:51:44.390
<v Clark Eichman>The damn sun is coming up already.
<v Clark Eichman>该死的太阳已经出来了。

00:51:44.390 --> 00:52:00.270
<v Ira Glass>The damn sun streaks its damn light through the cursed windows. Donna straightens things up a little, surveys the restaurant. She's the waitress that we first interviewed a full day before, the one who brings in cookies on Christmas for everybody. She eyes the morning regulars at the tables.
<v Ira Glass>该死的太阳透过诅咒的窗户投下它该死的光。唐娜稍微整理了一下，打量着餐厅。她就是我们整整一天前采访的第一位服务员，那个圣诞节给大家带饼干的人。她看着桌边的早晨常客。

00:52:00.270 --> 00:53:47.850
<v Donna>Well, it's now 5 o'clock Saturday morning. I got one hour and 45 minutes. And I know it sounds a little corny, but I really do enjoy it. As soon as it starts to get daylight, I start to feel good. The day people come in, the nice smells, nice colognes. [LAUGHS] You know, it's kind of wore off on the night people, but the day people, it's so fresh. It's nice, refreshing. Everybody else is getting sleepy and I'm starting to wake up because I'm a day person that's been working nights for 26 years. [LAUGHS] But I'm handling it all right.
<v Donna>嗯，现在是周六凌晨5点。我还有一小时45分钟。我知道这听起来有点俗气，但我真的很享受它。一旦天开始亮，我就开始感觉很好。白天的人进来，好闻的味道，好闻的香水。[笑声]你知道，这对晚上的人来说有点消退了，但白天的人，它是那么新鲜。很好，令人振奋。其他人开始想睡了，而我开始醒过来，因为我是个上了26年夜班的日班人。[笑声]但我应付得来。

